Drop nixpkgs' "ambiguous ABI" assertion for the `gnu` ABI on big-endian
64-bit PowerPC, so `crossSystem.config = "powerpc64-linux-gnu"` — GUIX's
exact target triple for the powerpc64 Bitcoin Core release — elaborates.

With the plain `gnu` ABI, nixpkgs' platform-flags pass NO
--with-abi/--with-cpu/--with-long-double-* to the cross gcc (abis.gnu
carries no attrs), which is exactly GUIX's bare gcc configure; gcc's own
built-in default for powerpc64-linux-gnu is ELFv1, matching the upstream
release binaries (e_flags 0x1 "abiv1", interpreter /lib64/ld64.so.1).
nixpkgs' own parse default for ABI-less BE-ppc64 triples is gnuabielfv1
for the same reason — the assertion only rejects the EXPLICIT "gnu"
spelling, which is the spelling the GUIX triple needs.

Applied via pkgs.applyPatches to the pinned nixpkgs in default.nix; only
the powerpc64 package set uses the patched copy.

--- a/lib/systems/parse.nix
+++ b/lib/systems/parse.nix
@@ -703,12 +703,6 @@
             The "gnu" ABI is ambiguous on 32-bit ARM. Use "gnueabi" or "gnueabihf" instead.
           '';
         }
-        {
-          assertion = platform: !(platform.isPower64 && platform.isBigEndian);
-          message = ''
-            The "gnu" ABI is ambiguous on big-endian 64-bit PowerPC. Use "gnuabielfv2" or "gnuabielfv1" instead.
-          '';
-        }
       ];
     };
     gnuabi64 = {
